WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.930
In the last video, you learned about the GRU, the gated recurring unit, and how that

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:09.970
can allow you to learn very long-range connections in a sequence.

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:14.410
The other type of unit that allows you to do this very well is the LSTM, or the Long

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:19.170
Short-Term Memory Unit, and this is even more powerful than the GRU.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.170
Let's take a look.

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:23.770
Here are the equations from the previous video for the GRU.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:29.610
And for the GRU, we had AT equals CT, and two gates, the update gate and the

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:35.210
relevance gate, CTILTER T, which is a candidate for replacing the memory cell,

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:41.530
and then we used the update gate gamma U to decide whether or not to update CT

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:43.770
using CTILTER T.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:50.650
The LSTM is an even slightly more powerful and more general version of the GRU,

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:56.570
and is due to Seb Hochreiter and Jurgen Schmidt Huber, and this was a really

00:00:56.570 --> 00:01:00.730
seminal paper that had a huge impact on sequence modeling.

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:05.210
Although I think this paper is one of the more difficult ones to read, it goes

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:09.450
quite a lot into the theory of vanishing gradients, and so I think more

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:13.810
people have learned about the details of LSTM through maybe other places than

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:17.450
from this particular paper, even though I think this paper has had a wonderful

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:19.810
impact on the deep learning community.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:23.330
But these are the equations that govern the LSTM.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:30.130
So we'll continue to the memory cell C, and the candidate value for updating it,

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:33.170
CILTER T, will be this.

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:45.170
And so notice that for the LSTM, we will no longer have the case that AT is equal to

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:46.170
CT.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.370
So this is what we'll use.

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:51.770
And so this is just like the equation on the left, except that we've now more

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:56.850
explicitly used AT there, or AT minus 1 instead of CT minus 1, and we're not

00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:01.530
using this gamma R, this relevance gate, although you can have a variation of

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:05.290
the LSTM where you put that back in, but maybe the more common version of

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:08.530
the LSTM doesn't bother with that.

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:15.250
And then we will have an update gate, same as before, so W update, and I'm

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:22.450
going to use AT minus 1 here, CT plus U.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:28.810
And one new property of the LSTM is instead of having one update gate control

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:32.570
both of these terms, we're going to have two separate terms.

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:38.010
So instead of gamma U and 1 minus gamma U, we're going to have gamma U here

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:42.490
and forget gate, which we're going to call gamma F.

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:50.570
So this gate gamma F is going to be sigmoid of pretty much what you'd expect,

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:59.930
XT plus BF, and then we're going to have a new output gate, which is sigma of

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:12.210
WO, and then again pretty much what you'd expect, plus BO, and then the

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:18.290
update value to the memory cell will be CT equals gamma U, then this

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:23.050
asterisk denotes element-wise multiplication, there's a vector vector

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:27.850
element-wise multiplication, plus, and instead of 1 minus gamma U, we're

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:34.890
going to have a separate forget gate, gamma F times C of T minus 1.

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:39.970
So this gives the memory cell the option of keeping the old value, CT minus

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:45.730
1, and then just adding to it this new value, C tilde of T. So use a separate

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:57.970
update and forget gates. So this stands for update forget and output gates.

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:06.450
And then finally, instead of AT equals CT, AT is going to equal to the output gate,

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:14.210
element-wise multiply with CT. So these are the equations that govern the LSTM,

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:19.570
and you can tell it has three gates instead of two, so it's a bit more

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:24.210
complicated, and it places the gates in slightly different places. So here again

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:30.970
are the equations governing the behavior of the LSTM. Once again, it's

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:35.090
traditional to explain these things using pictures, so let me draw one here,

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:40.290
and if these pictures are too complicated, don't worry about it. I personally find the

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:44.610
equations easier to understand than the picture, but I'll just show the picture

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:48.690
here for the intuitions it conveys. The particular picture here was very much

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:53.890
inspired by a blog post due to Chris Olar, titled Understanding LSTM Networks,

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:57.610
and the diagram drawn here is quite similar to one that he drew in his

00:04:57.610 --> 00:05:01.730
blog post. But the key things to take away from this picture are maybe that

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:07.490
you use AT minus 1 and XT to compute all the gate values. So in this picture,

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:12.250
you have AT minus 1 and XT coming together to compute the forget gate, to

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:17.930
compute the update gate, and to compute the output gate, and they also go

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:23.770
through a tannish to compute C tilde T, and then these values are combined in

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:30.410
these complicated ways with element-wise multiplies and so on to get CT from

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:36.090
the previous CT minus 1. Now one element of this is interesting is if you hook up a

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:39.970
bunch of these in parallel, so that's one of them, and you connect them, you know,

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:46.050
connect these temporally. So this is the input X1, then X2, X3, so you can take

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:53.730
these units and just hook them up as follows, where the output A from a

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:57.690
previous time step is the input A at the next time step, and similarly for C.

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:02.130
And I've simplified the diagrams a little bit at the bottom. And one cool thing about this,

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:08.210
you notice is that there's this line at the top that shows how, so long as you

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:13.970
set the forget and the update gates appropriately, it is relatively easy for

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:19.890
the LSTM to have some value C0 and have that be passed all the way to the

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:26.050
right to have, you know, maybe C3 equals C0. And this is why the LSTM, as well as

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:31.730
the GRU, is very good at memorizing certain values even for a long time, for

00:06:31.730 --> 00:06:36.610
certain real values stored in the memory cell even for many, many time steps.

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:47.120
So that's it for the LSTM. As you can imagine, there are also a few variations on

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:52.000
this that people use. Perhaps the most common one is that instead of just

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:58.240
having the gate values be dependent only on A, T minus 1, and XT, sometimes people

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:07.720
also sneak in there the value C, T minus 1 as well. This is called a peephole

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:13.920
connection. Not a great name maybe, but you see peephole connection. What that

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:20.240
means is that the gate values may depend not just on AT minus 1 and on XT, but

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:25.320
also on the previous memory cell value. And the peephole connection can go into

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:31.320
all three of these gates computations. So that's one common variation you see of

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:38.320
LSTMs. One technical detail is that these are, say, hundred dimensional vectors. If

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:45.480
you have a hundred dimensional hidden memory cell unit, so is this. And the, say,

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:50.120
fifth element of CT minus 1 affects only the fifth element of the

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:55.720
corresponding gates. So that relationship is one to one where not every element of

00:07:55.720 --> 00:08:00.040
the hundred dimensional CT minus 1 can affect all elements of the gates. But

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:04.680
instead, the first element of CT minus 1 affects the first element of the gates,

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:09.120
the second element affects the second element, and so on. But if you ever read the paper and see

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:12.840
someone talk about a peephole connection, that's what they mean. That CT

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:19.200
minus 1 is used to affect the gate value as well. So that's it for the LSTM.

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:24.480
When should you use a GRU and when should you use an LSTM? There isn't widespread

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:30.000
consensus in this. And even though I presented GRUs first, in the history of

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.920
deep learning, LSTMs actually came much earlier. And then GRUs were relatively

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:39.320
recent invention that were maybe derived as partly a simplification of the

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:44.040
more complicated LSTM model. Researchers have tried both of these models on many

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:47.640
different problems, and on different problems, different algorithms will win

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:51.960
out. So there isn't a universally superior algorithm, which is why I want to

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:57.840
show you both of them. But I feel like when I am using these, the advantage of

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:02.200
the GRU is that it's a simpler model, and so it's actually easier to build a

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:06.720
much bigger network than only a 2 gate. So computationally, it runs a bit faster,

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:11.440
so it scales to building somewhat bigger models. But the LSTM is

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:17.320
more powerful and more flexible, since it has 3 gates instead of 2. If you want to pick one

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:22.040
to use, I think LSTM has been the historically more proven choice, so if you

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:26.520
had to pick one, I think most people today would still use the LSTM as the

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:31.480
default first thing to try. Although I think in the last few years, GRUs have

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:35.160
been gaining a lot of momentum, and I feel like more and more teams are

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:39.480
also using GRUs because they're a bit simpler, but often were just as

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:44.440
well, and it might be easier to scale them to even bigger problems. So that's

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:49.720
it for LSTMs. With either GRUs or LSTMs, you'll be able to build new

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:55.160
networks that can capture much longer range dependencies.
